WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 64: The Hatfield-McCoy Feud: Blood and Timber in the Tug Valley

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:17.440
[Rebecca] In the steep, shadowed hollows of the Tug River Valley along the border of Kentucky and West Virginia, two proud mountain clans waged the most famous and mythologized family feud in American history.

4
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.600
[Rebecca] The Hatfield McCoy Vendetta.

5
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:32.480
[Rebecca] What popular culture remembers as a cartoonish brawl over a stolen pig was, in reality, a deadly clash of civil war allegiances, industrial timber monopolies, political corruption, and frontier vengeance.

6
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:43.120
[Rebecca] Over nearly three decades, the feud claimed a dozen lives, brought two sovereign states to the brink of armed warfare, and culminated in a sensational midnight house burning.

7
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.920
[Rebecca] George tells the true, tragic story of the Hatfields and McCoys.

8
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:05.760
[George] In the 1860s, the valley was dominated by two powerful, sprawling extended clans.

9
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:12.080
[George] On the West Virginia side were the Hatfields, commanded by William Anderson Devil Ants Hatfield.

10
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:28.480
[George] Devil Ants was a towering, heavily bearded six-foot mountaineer with sharp grey eyes, an expert marksman, and a shrewd, highly successful timber merchant who owned thousands of acres of Virgin Mountain timber and employed dozens of armed clansmen in his logging crews.

11
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.640
[George] Across the river on the Kentucky side lived the McCoys, led by Randolph O'Ranald McCoy.

12
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:42.560
[George] Randolph was an austere, brooding, deeply religious patriarch who farmed and operated a small gristmill on Blackberry Creek.

13
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.760
[George] During the American Civil War, deep ideological fractures divided the families.

14
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:57.840
[George] Devil Ants Hatfield and his brothers had served as Confederate cavalrymen in the Logan County Wildcats, while Randolph McCoy and several sons served in the Union Army.

15
00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:06.920
[George] In 1865, Randolph's brother Harmon McCoy, who had returned home after serving in the Union infantry, was murdered in an ambush.

16
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:17.480
[George] Although Devil Ants's fiery Confederate uncle, Jim Vance, was widely suspected, no charges were ever filed, planting the first bitter seeds of clan resentment.

17
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:28.440
[George] The steep hollows of the Tug Valley were thick with ancient virgin timber, and both clans operated logging rafts that floated valuable oak and walnut logs downriver to market.

18
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:37.080
[George] Thirteen years later, in the autumn of 1878, the smoldering hostility exploded into the open over a single razorback hog.

19
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:45.640
[George] In the Appalachian Mountains, where livestock roamed free on the open range, pigs were identified by unique notches cut into their ears.

20
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:56.520
[George] Randolph McCoy visited the farm of Floyd Hatfield, a cousin of Devil Ant's, and spotted an ear-notched hog in Hatfield's pen that Randolph adamantly claimed belonged to him.

21
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:02.680
[George] Floyd Hatfield insisted the pig was his, and Randolph brought formal legal charges of theft.

22
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:09.960
[George] The matter was tried before the local justice of the peace, Anderson Preacher Ants Hatfield, Devil Ants's brother.

23
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.680
[George] The trial took place before a six-man Hatfield and six-man McCoy jury.

24
00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:21.320
[George] The decisive witness was Bill Staten, a nephew of Randolph McCoy who was married to a Hatfield woman.

25
00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:26.280
[George] Staten testified under oath that the hog's ear notches were Floyd Hatfield's marks.

26
00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.160
[George] Based on Staten's testimony, the jury ruled in favor of the Hatfields.

27
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:37.240
[George] Randolph McCoy was humiliated and enraged, regarding Staten as a treacherous Judas.

28
00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:45.440
[George] Two years later, two of Randolph's nephews, Sam and Paris McCoy, ambushed Bill Staten in the woods, shooting him dead.

29
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:52.240
[George] Although tried for murder, the McCoy brothers were acquitted on grounds of self-defense, deepening the thirst for vengeance.

30
00:03:52.480 --> 00:04:03.120
[George] The hog trial was held in a frontier cabin crowded with armed partisans, where every word of testimony was weighed with deadly gravity by two suspicious mountain families.

31
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:14.880
[George] Early settlers faced immense hardships carving farms and homesteads from the dense eastern woodlands, battling disease, inclement weather, and frontier isolation with unyielding resolve.

32
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.000
[George] The feud took a romantic and tragic turn on an election day in August 1880.

33
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:29.120
[George] Johns Hatfield, Devil Ants's handsome, reckless 18-year-old son, crossed the river to attend a Kentucky political rally and dance.

34
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.200
[George] There he met Rosanna McCoy, Randolph's beautiful, spirited twenty-year-old daughter.

35
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:44.160
[George] The two fell instantly in love and slipped away into the mountains, with Rosanna defying her family to move into the Hatfield homestead in West Virginia.

36
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:52.000
[George] Randolph McCoy was beside himself with fury, viewing his daughter's relationship with a Hatfield as the ultimate betrayal.

37
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:03.360
[George] Months later, when Johns Hatfield ventured into Kentucky, a heavily armed McCoy posse ambushed him and took him prisoner, planning to transport him to the Pikeville jail on Old Warrants.

38
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:14.720
[George] Learning of her lover's capture, Rosanna mounted a horse bareback, galloped through the night across the river to alert Devil Ants, who mobilized an armed squad of twenty Hatfield riflemen.

39
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:22.480
[George] The Hatfields intercepted the McCoy posse on the road, leveled their Winchesters, and rescued Johns without firing a shot.

40
00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:33.800
[George] Yet, despite Rosanna's courage, Johns eventually abandoned her, marrying her cousin Nancy McCoy, breaking Rosanna's heart and leaving the families more bitterly divided than ever.

41
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:44.360
[George] Rosanna McCoy lived out her remaining years in quiet grief, remembered by Valley elders as a brave, tenderhearted soul caught between two unyielding mountain empires.

42
00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:55.320
[George] The spirit of American innovation was evident in every workshop and meeting hall, where practical mechanics and visionary thinkers sought solutions to the most challenging obstacles of their era.

43
00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:04.280
[George] The point of no return arrived on August 7, 1882, during another rowdy election day near Blackberry Creek, Kentucky.

44
00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:14.920
[George] An argument broke out between Devil Antts's younger brother, Ellison Hatfield, and three of Randolph McCoy's sons, Talbert, Farmer, and young Randolph J.R.

45
00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.840
[George] Fueled by moonshine whiskey, the brawl turned violent.

46
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.480
[George] Talbert McCoy drew a pocket knife, stabbing Ellison multiple times.

47
00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:30.920
[George] As Ellison fought back, pinning Talbert to the ground, Farmer McCoy pulled a revolver and shot Ellison through the lungs.

48
00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:36.840
[George] Kentucky constables arrested the three McCoy brothers, planning to march them to Pikeville for trial.

49
00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:49.080
[George] But Devil Ants Hatfield and his armed clansmen intercepted the deputies, disarmed them, and took the three McCoy boys back across the river to West Virginia, locking them in an abandoned schoolhouse.

50
00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:56.200
[George] Devil Ants issued a cold ultimatum if Ellison lived, the McCoy boys would be surrendered to Kentucky courts.

51
00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.400
[George] If Ellison died, they would pay with their lives.

52
00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.160
[George] Two days later, Ellison Hatfield succumbed to his wounds.

53
00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:16.160
[George] On the night of August 9, the Hatfields marched the three bound McCoy brothers back to the Kentucky bank of the Tug River, tied them to pawpaw bushes, and executed them with a firing squad of over fifty rifle shots.

54
00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:26.000
[George] The execution at the Pawpaw Bushes cast a permanent pall of dread over the Tug Valley, forcing families to sleep with loaded rifles across their knees for years.

55
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:38.160
[George] News of these extraordinary events traveled by telegraph and steam train across the nation, captivating readers in major eastern cities and inspiring future generations of pioneers and inventors.

56
00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.040
[George] The brutal execution of the three McCoy boys shocked the state of Kentucky.

57
00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:56.640
[George] A brilliant, ambitious Pikeville attorney named Perry Klein, who was related to the McCoys and held an old financial grievance against Devil Ants Hatfield over a disputed timber land settlement, launched a legal crusade.

58
00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:10.160
[George] Klein used his political influence in the state capital of Frankfurt to convince Kentucky Governor Simon Bolivar Buckner to issue formal extradition warrants and post massive bounties for devil ants and twenty Hatfield clansmen.

59
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:21.680
[George] To hunt down the Hatfields, Klein recruited bad Frank Phillips, a fearless, ruthless former deputy sheriff who organized a heavily armed paramilitary posse of bounty hunters.

60
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:32.960
[George] Phillips launched illegal cross-border raids into West Virginia, raiding Hatfield farms, engaging in running gun battles, and kidnapping clan members to collect the state bounties.

61
00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:34.960
[George] West Virginia Governor E.

62
00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:43.760
[George] Willis Wilson retaliated by deploying the West Virginia state militia to protect his citizens, threatening to march armed troops into Kentucky.

63
00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:52.240
[George] The two sovereign American states were on the verge of interstate civil war, with federal marshals struggling to maintain peace along the border.

64
00:08:52.240 --> 00:09:02.440
[George] Governors in Frankfurt and Charleston traded angry diplomatic dispatches, while sensational yellow journalism in New York painted the mountain people as lawless savages.

65
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:13.640
[George] The enduring lessons of this historic chapter continue to resonate across American culture, reminding us of the courage, sacrifice and vision of those who shaped the destiny of our nation.

66
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:26.040
[George] Desperate to end the legal harassment once and for all, a rogue faction of the Hatfield clan, led by the ruthless cap Hatfield and Uncle Jim Vance, decided to wipe out the McCoy family leadership.

67
00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:36.360
[George] In the freezing early morning hours of January 1, 1888, the Hatfield squad surrounded Randolph McCoy's isolated log cabin on Blackberry Creek.

68
00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:42.200
[George] When Randolph refused to surrender, the raiders set the cabin roof on fire with torches.

69
00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:48.040
[George] As the family rushed into the yard in their nightclothes through the smoke, the gunmen opened fire.

70
00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:57.960
[George] Randolph's grown daughter Alifair was shot through the heart, his son Calvin was cut down by rifle fire, and his wife Sarah was bludgeoned with a shotgun barrel.

71
00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:06.840
[George] Leaving her skull fractured, Randolph McCoy managed to shoot his way through the circle of fire, escaping barefoot into the snowy woods.

72
00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:17.000
[George] The cold-blooded murder of innocent women and children repulsed public opinion across the nation, and bad Frank Phillips's posse relentlessly hunted down the perpetrators.

73
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:25.320
[George] Jim Vance was cornered in the mountains and killed in a shootout, and eight Hatfields were captured and brought in heavy chains to the Pikeville jail.

74
00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:34.760
[George] The New Year massacre marked the tragic nader of the conflict, demonstrating the catastrophic cost of unbridled vengeance when law and reason are abandoned.

75
00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:40.520
[George] The feud culminated in 1889 when the Supreme Court of the United States ruled in Man v.

76
00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:48.560
[George] Justice that Kentucky had legal jurisdiction to try the captured Hatfields, even though they had been illegally kidnapped from West Virginia soil.

77
00:10:48.800 --> 00:11:07.120
[George] In 1890, following high-profile trials in Pikeville, seven Hatfields were sentenced to life imprisonment, and Ellison Mounts, believed to be Ellison Hatfield's illegitimate son who had shot Alifair McCoy, was publicly hanged before a crowd of thousands on the courthouse square in Pikeville.

78
00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:23.760
[George] Devil Ants Hatfield, recognizing that the era of frontier clannish isolation was ending as coal and railroad companies bought up the mountains, retired to an isolated homestead on Island Creek, West Virginia, where he was baptized in a mountain stream at age 73.

79
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:29.040
[George] In 1921, Devil Ants passed away peacefully of pneumonia at 81.

80
00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.920
[George] A life-sized Carrara marble statue imported from Italy marks his grave.

81
00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:54.080
[George] Finally, in June 2003, over 60 descendants of both families gathered in Pikeville to sign an official proclamation of peace, formally ending the bloodiest family feud in American lore and proving that even the deepest historical hatreds can ultimately yield to forgiveness, shared heritage, and peace.

82
00:11:54.080 --> 00:12:04.160
[George] Today, descendants of the Hatfields and McCoys celebrate their shared Appalachian heritage together, proving that reconciliation and forgiveness are always possible.

83
00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:19.520
[Rebecca] Join us next week for another compelling journey into the hidden chapters of our past.

